XERJ - first start, service use, and security
=============================================

XERJ is a local search engine and MCP server.  The package installs the
`xerj` binary and an example configuration at:

    /usr/local/share/examples/xerj/xerj.toml

There is no default configuration search path.  A server must be given
`--config /path/to/xerj.toml` (or use XERJ_CONFIG); a bare `xerj` uses
compiled defaults and creates `./data` in the current directory.

Authentication and TLS
----------------------

Upstream defaults are authentication ON and TLS OFF.  The first start
creates an admin key at `<data_dir>/admin.key` with mode 0600.  Every
client must send that key, including `xerj autoindex`.  Do not use
`--insecure`: it disables both authentication and TLS.  Keep the default
loopback bind unless TLS or a TLS-terminating proxy is in front of every
listener.

The gRPC listener on port 8081 is cleartext h2c even when the REST
listeners use TLS.  Do not expose port 8081 outside a trusted host or a
TLS-terminating proxy.  A self-signed certificate is generated only when
TLS is enabled without certificate paths; supply a real certificate for
any non-local service.

Data and model downloads
------------------------

The data directory is supplied by the administrator and should be
writable only by the account running XERJ.  Back it up as sensitive
data: it contains the admin key, session material and index contents.

The package keeps upstream's default `neural` and `decide-local`
features, but the default embedding mode is lexical.  A neural model
(~90 MB) is downloaded only when `--embed-mode neural` is selected (or
configured), and is cached outside the data directory.  Pre-download it
or use a local model directory if the service has no outbound network.

No rc.d service or dedicated user is installed in this initial port.
Runtime permissions, HOME-dependent pointer files, signal handling and
pledge/unveil behaviour still need validation on OpenBSD.
